{{also|Bonnie}}
==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|bonny}}
* {{l|en|bonie}} {{qualifier|obsolete}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|*boni}} (attested only rarely as {{m|enm|bon}}, {{m|enm|boun}}), probably from {{der|en|fro|bon}}, feminine {{m|fro|bonne||[[good]]}}, from {{der|en|la|bonus||good}}. See {{m|en|bounty}}, and compare {{m|en|bonus}}, {{m|en|boon}}.

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# [[merry]]; [[happy]];
#: {{syn|en|frolicsome|cheerful|blithe|gay}}
#* ''c. 1598 or 1599'', [[w:William Shakespeare|William Shakespeare]], ''Much Ado About Nothing''
#*: ''Be you blithe and '''bonny'''''<br>Converting all your sounds of woe<br>Into Hey nonny, nonny.
# {{lb|en|Geordie|Scotland}} [[beautiful|Beautiful]]; [[pretty]]; [[attractive]].
# {{lb|en|dialectal|Scotland|Northern England}} [[fine|Fine]], [[good]] (often used ironically).
#: {{ux|en|My '''bonnie''' friend, come over here.}}

